welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Chloe Starr assistant professor of  Asian Christianity and theology at Yale  University she has published on Chinese  literature and Chinese theology and her  recent works include red light novels of  the late Ching a co edited volume the  quest for gentility in China and an  edited volume reading Christian  scriptures in China today we talk with  her about her forthcoming book Chinese  intellectual Christianity welcome  professor star thank you let's begin  with an overview of your book tell us  about it so the book Chinese  intellectual Christianity traces the  currents the major currents of Chinese  theology and some of the main figures  who've been writing Chinese theology  starting in the late Imperial period in  the Jesuit era when the Jesuits were  there and the theologians were Chinese  literati moving through the late Qing to  the Republican era and then on through  the 20th century into the beginning of  the 21st century okay and what led you  to write the book um several things  first of all it's hugely interesting  especially Republican era theology it's  one of my favorite periods of theology  when there's a huge flourishing of  thinking about what it means to have a  Chinese theology that isn't tied too  much to Western thought and thinking but  is really you know engaged with Chinese  society and Chinese ways of thinking and  writing um one of the reasons I wanted  to write this book was for an  english-speaking audience there's very  little out there that's Chinese theology  in English there's very little good  academic work and so when I teach  courses I ascribe readings another  reason I wanted to write the book was  for Chinese speaking audience there's  been a lot of work over the last decade  or so revising the history we have of  Chinese Christianity was a whole period  from around the 1930s to the 1980s or so  when Christianity was wiped out of the  record whether it's the history Doria  graphical record or whether it's the  Chinese literature  record and what thinkers are doing now  is rien scribing Christianity that was  always there into the record so people  are working on the Chinese Christian  colleges from the nineteen ten a lot of  historians are opening up new archives  and rethinking Christianity in that  period in a more positive sense than was  possible during the Communist era okay  so that's why it was wiped out because  it was during the time of communism in  China yeah there's one more reason which  is really important to why I wanted to  write the book and that's because the  Chinese Church is essentially being  split since the 1950s into the house  Church the underground Church and the  state church and the house church people  who a lot of whom spent decades in  prison for their stance on Christianity  and the government still regard the  mainstream the state church in the  Protestant side the three-self principal  movement church as communist Stooges or  people who capitulated and worked with  the communist government not not somehow  Christian so one of the things I want to  do and I think in a way it's almost  easier as an outsider is to go back to  the beginning of some of the debates in  the 1950s and look at the figures  involved so ding guangtian on the state  church side somebody like warming down  on the house church side and actually  see revisit those arguments and and see  if there's a way of reconciling their  theological views or reconciling their  relation to the government from our  present position to sort of rethink this  huge split in the church ok so again the  time frame of your book starts when and  ends where it starts in the sixteenth  seventeenth century with some of the  Chinese thinkers so it's there's four  phases in the book and the the sort of  the model of the book is tied to the  flourishing periods of Chinese  Christianity so it's not tied to Mission  history it's not tied to secular history  it focuses in on the exciting  theological periods so the first one is  late Imperial when you have the three  pillars of the church in the Ming  Dynasty  who are rethinking and interacting with  their Jesuit priest friends and teachers  and beginning to think about writing  apologetics or Chinese Christian works  and the second phase is in the early  20th century in the Republican era 1920s  and 30s you had a lot of Chinese  students going abroad so we had people  studying at Columbia at Union  Theological Seminary in the US and then  going back to China and teaching so a  lot of the theologians in this period  were university teachers and lectures  and these are sort of some of the  intellectuals of my title but they're  also hugely socially engaged and so the  theology of this period is looking at  you know what can the church do for  China and that's true even in the later  period this is sort of it so the  theological stream I'm looking at is not  really so much the house church or the  independent Chinese churches but the  stream of theologians who were in the  sort of Chinese literati tradition  really deeply engaged with society and  with Chinese society and we're trying to  think theologically about what it means  to be Chinese huge questions in the  1920s were about identity who are we as  Chinese Christians because you've got  westernization and modernization going  on you've got a huge anti-christian  movement you've got a pro-science  movement so the theologians in the 1920s  are trying to write an apologetics so  you get a very rational theology very  few miracles in there but they're also  trying to create a social gospel how can  we bring the kingdom of heaven on earth  now in China in the 1920s and 30s how  popular was Christianity you know  throughout those time periods I mean  Christianity and perhaps I'm wrong in  this was basically brought to China by  missionaries is that correct  the Nestorian Church Church of the East  Syria speaking church have missionaries  monks in China in the seventh century  we've got documentary evidence from  around 65 ad okay but that sort of phase  of Christianity died out and are the  prohibitions on Christianity in the  ninth century in the late tang and then  you have the Franciscans and then you  have the Jesuits and then you have the  Protestant mission era the heyday in the  sort of 19th century of sending huge  numbers of missionaries to China  percentage-wise has never been so many  Christians in China I mean even now  estimates between 60 and 100 million  Chinese Christians okay so tell me about  how you're doing your  research and I'm wondering about like  you know language as well how that fits  into what you're doing so almost all of  the materials were all materials I'm  using almost all of the materials I'm  using are in Chinese so they're either  in classical Chinese from the early  period or they're in modern Chinese from  the 20th century and one of the things  I'm trying to do in the book is is  create a narrative that moves through  not many texts have actually integrated  classical Chinese writings of a  theological nature with modern ones and  integrated Protestant and Catholic works  so that's one of the things I'm trying  to do but by looking at text of  individual authors so each section of  the volume has a historical a background  chapter and then it'll have two or three  chapters going into detail looking at  the text of individual theologians so I  spend a lot of time photocopying in Hong  Kong in a couple of years ago in the  libraries and I've also talked to  various Roman Catholic theologians and  friends about who is the most  interesting 20th century Roman Catholic  theologian and then they gave me all  these names of Taiwanese and I said no i  need mainland people I'm interested in  mainland Chinese thinking so and was  there a general opinion about who that  was or it's difficult on the Roman  Catholic side because there hasn't been  so much original thinking it's been  monly trammeled by Vatican currents of  thought on the Protestant side you have  a lot more because the the movement  towards a Chinese Chinese church towards  independence and autonomy happened in  the 1910s and 20s at the same time as  huge numbers of independent churches  were growing up and also with these  returnees theologians coming back from  the u.s. newly trained up newly excited  with this of new critical thinking  writing sort of innovative Chinese  theology okay and you know during the  process of your research did you come  across anything that particularly  surprised you that you weren't expecting  perhaps one of the things that's been  most fun I did literature in my early  degrees in Chinese is the way that some  of the Chinese theological writings are  really using Chinese literary forms to  say new things about Christianity so I  have a chapter on Dad's a ton who's a  theologian who was writing in the 1920s  and 30s and he likes a bi  a life of Jesus now there are many lives  of Jesus in by Western writers at that  time in his one he uses Chinese poems  from the classical corpus as chapter  titles for example and he uses so many  images from you know four character  phrases and all these sort of Chinese  images and he reads the text in a  Chinese way and then he reads the life  of cheezer's as a Chinese literati Award  so for example one of the things he's  doing he reads the landscape of Nazareth  and Judea as if he were a sort of  Chinese poet standing there reading this  layered landscape with all the memories  built into it so we get we get a sort of  Chinese take on the gospel in bad and  another of the writers I'm looking at at  the moment in the same period as  shuzhong he's a Roman Catholic writer  and he wrote these diary entries it's  called the BG form in Chinese and it's  basically jottings it's a bit like a  blog or even some of it's like a retweet  so I I see this thing in a newspaper and  I'm telling you about it and he he  published these in a church magazine  over three years and then he collected  them into this book but the diary form  is a very Chinese form of writing so the  things he says in that and the sort of  theological thoughts he has are a deeply  Chinese and are not really for a Western  readership because they seem they seem  very unsystematic none of the  theologians I'm writing actually write  systematic theology in western terms so  it's very tied to Chinese literary forms  and Chinese ways of thinking and I think  that's something that hasn't been  explored so much before interesting and  what what are some of the conclusions  that you reach in the book one of the  sort of most interesting things about  twentieth-century and Chinese theology  is how closely the church and the state  are tied and how closely mainstream  theologians are working with and  thinking through some of the Communist  requirements on the state but but even  even in the nationalist area at the 20s  and 30s  how what Christianity can do for China  is one of the main currents of thought  and you see that again in the final  Christian theology of the 1990s and  2000's how can we leverage this  Christian theology to have some  use in the public sphere to have some  sort of ethical effect in China that's  one of the questions a lot of the  academic theologians are thinking about  at the moment so what are some of the  examples of things that Christianity can  do for China that you've read in your  research so at one level of governments  are very positive about some aspects of  Protestantism at the moment in the  spiritual vacuum and void in the post  cultural revolution era and the  generation that's grown up without a  value set so some of the Christian  academic theologians are thinking about  okay how can Christian theological  ethics be brought into national life and  some of the public intellectuals are  wanting to bring Christianity into  public discourse and within the academic  sphere to use the methodology of  theology as a counterpoint to some of  the sort of Marxian humanistic  methodologies okay well when can we look  for to your book coming out I'm hoping  they'll be out in 2015 okay very good  fascinating thank you for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work thank you so much thank you for  more information about Professor star  and her research please visit our  website at Yale edu / Macmillan report  be sure to join us again for another  episode of the Macmillan report made  possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

